A. Upon presentation of identification to the lodging provider, business owner, property owner, wholesale alcohol dealer, retail alcohol dealer, alcohol license owner, telephone company, insurance company, financial institution, or any other person or business relevant to the provisions of this title (hereinafter collectively "business"), any employee or agent of the city is hereby authorized to make inspections of a business's books, papers, records, financial reports, computer media, data storage media, equipment and other facilities in order to verify the accuracy of any revenues, financial reports, financial returns, documents, and taxes, for the purposes of auditing said financial records to determine if there are any monies owed by the business to the city and to determine whether the business is in compliance with the requirements of this chapter.
B. Inspections shall be carried out during regular business hours, unless an emergency exists. The inspection warrant shall be effective for the entire time specified therein, but not for a period of more than fourteen (14) calendar days, unless extended or renewed by the judicial officer who signed and issued the original inspection warrant, upon satisfying himself that such extension or renewal is in the public interest. After the expiration of such time, the inspection warrant, unless executed, is void. The city's employees and agents may carry out such inspection pursuant to the inspection warrant each and every day during the fourteen-day period without requiring the city to secure any additional inspection warrants.
C. The city clerk or their designee, in addition to other procedures provided by law, may obtain an inspection warrant under the conditions specified in this section. The inspection warrant shall authorize the city clerk or their designee to conduct a search or inspection of property without the consent of the person whose property is to be searched or inspected, under the conditions set out in this section.
D. Inspection warrants may be issued by any judge of the municipal court when the issuing judge is satisfied that the following conditions are met:
1. The person seeking the inspection warrant must establish under oath or affirmation that there is probable cause for believing that the business is not in compliance with the requirements of this title;
2. The issuing judge determines that the issuance of the warrant is authorized by this title and all other applicable law;
3. The inspection warrant is attached to a supporting affidavit which particularly describes the property to be inspected and particularly describes the purpose for which the inspection is to be made;
4. The inspection warrant describes, either directly or by reference to the affidavit, the property upon which the inspection is to occur and is sufficiently accurate that the executor of the warrant and the owner or possessor of the property can reasonably determine from the warrant the property for which the warrant authorizes a search or inspection;
5. The warrant indicates the conditions, objects, activities, or circumstances that the search or inspection is intended to check or reveal; and
6. The warrant refers in general terms, to the provisions of the code or state law sought to be enforced.
7. It shall be unlawful for any business, operator or employee of the business to refuse to allow an inspection pursuant to an inspection warrant that has been issued in accordance with this section.
(Ord. dated 8/18/25 § 1)

490:
A. Any animal at-large or in violation of this chapter may be impounded in a humane manner. The owner must claim the animal within three business days (which shall not include Sundays or holidays observed by animal control), and is liable for all costs during impoundment, plus all other expenses of the city or animal control including, but not limited to, veterinarian fees. All costs shall be paid prior to the owner retrieving said animal, or upon disposal of such animal.
B. The following may be impounded, but are not limited solely to these:
1. Animals running at-large;
2. Animals, the ownership of which is unknown;
3. Female dogs/cats in heat and off the premises of their owners;
4. Animals which have bitten a person or animal, or been bitten by animal suspected of rabies;
5. Dogs/cats not vaccinated for rabies;
6. Dogs/cats which have strayed from the premises of their owners;
7. Dogs/cats suspected of having rabies; and
8. Unconfined dogs/cats in quarantine areas.
C. If an animal has not been reclaimed within three business days (as defined above), it shall become the property of animal control, which may convey ownership to any responsible person on conditions animal control may prescribe, or may humanely destroy such animal.
(Ord. dated 2/19/24, § 1)

547:
A. No inoperative vehicle, as that term is defined in subsection B. of this section, shall be parked or stand on any private property or public roads for longer than thirty (30) days after written notice to remove such vehicle has been delivered by the city to the record owner of such vehicle or to the owner of the property on which such vehicle is located. Such vehicles are hereby determined to create conditions tending to reduce the value of private property, to promote blight and deterioration, to invite plundering and vandalism, to create fire hazards, to constitute an attractive nuisance creating a hazard to the health and safety of minors, to create a harborage for rodents and insects, and to be injurious to the health, safety and general welfare of the public and, when on city streets, to create a traffic hazard and endanger public safety. This section shall not be the exclusive regulation of abandoned, wrecked, dismantled, or inoperative vehicles within the corporate limits of the city, but shall be supplemental and in addition to other regulations and regulatory codes, zoning ordinances, statutes, or provisions of law heretofore and hereafter enacted by the city, county, state or other legal entity or agency having proper jurisdiction.
B. An inoperative condition shall include, but not be limited to, any automobile, truck, vehicle, mobile machinery or trailer of any kind or type, or part thereof, the condition of which is one or more of the following:
1. Wrecked;
2. Dismantled;
3. Partially dismantled;
4. Inoperative;
5. Abandoned;
6. Discarded; or
7. One which does not have a valid license plate attached hereto.
C. The following conditions will permit the parking or standing of an inoperative vehicle on private property within the corporate limits of the city, provided that:
1. Such vehicle shall be fully enclosed within a building in NR1, NR2 and NR3 zoning districts, provided the owner or occupant of the building is in the process of repairing or reconditioning the vehicle for his personal use.
2. Such vehicle shall not be stored for the purpose of salvage or parts, but shall be in the process of repair or reconditioning.
D. Whenever any public officer of the city has reasonable cause to believe that an inoperative vehicle is being unlawfully maintained, or allowed to exist on any premises within the corporate limits of the city, such officer may enter said premises at all reasonable times for the purpose of inspecting said vehicle; provided that if said premises is occupied the public officer shall present proper credentials to the occupant prior to a request for entry. If said premises is not occupied, the public officer shall make a reasonable effort to locate the owner or other person having control of the premises. If entry is refused, the public officer shall have recourse to every legal remedy to secure entry, including application for an inspection warrant from Covington Municipal Court.
(Ord. dated 10/17/11 §§ 1, 2; Ord. dated 7/7/11, § 1)
